1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,650
You've probably heard that
bacteria in your body outnumber

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:05,380
your own cells ten to one.

3
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:08,150
It's one of the most repeated
facts in biology.

4
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:09,690
And it's wrong.

5
00:00:09,958 --> 00:00:13,028
That ratio came from a rough
estimate back in the

6
00:00:13,038 --> 00:00:14,310
nineteen seventies.

7
00:00:14,458 --> 00:00:17,748
In twenty sixteen, scientists in
Israel recounted,

8
00:00:17,758 --> 00:00:19,718
using far better data.

9
00:00:20,083 --> 00:00:23,433
A typical adult has about thirty
trillion human cells,

10
00:00:23,443 --> 00:00:26,023
and about thirty-eight trillion
bacteria.

11
00:00:26,542 --> 00:00:27,702
That's not ten to one.

12
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:29,422
It's roughly one to one.

13
00:00:29,792 --> 00:00:32,832
Most of those bacteria live in
your large intestine.

14
00:00:33,512 --> 00:00:36,132
And most of your own cells are
red blood cells.

15
00:00:36,583 --> 00:00:39,883
The ratio even shifts every time
you go to the toilet.

16
00:00:40,503 --> 00:00:41,303
Afterwards,

17
00:00:41,583 --> 00:00:43,463
your human cells may briefly
win.

18
00:00:43,875 --> 00:00:45,295
So you're not mostly bacteria.

19
00:00:45,915 --> 00:00:47,175
It's about an even split.
